{"status": "success", "data": {"classes": [{"strm": 2895, "crseId": 370925, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "RUSSL", "catalogNbr": "2158", "titleShort": "St. Petersburg & Modern Russia", "titleLong": "St. Petersburg and the Making of Modern Russia", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 17662, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:30PM", "timeEnd": "04:10PM", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/06/2025", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "ol76", "firstName": "Olga", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Litvak"}], "pattern": "MW", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/17/2025", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "GRD", "gradingBasisShort": "Graded", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "HIST", "catalogNbr": "2158", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "SHUM", "catalogNbr": "2158", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/21/2025", "sessionEndDt": "05/17/2025", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "Founded by Peter the Great in the early eighteenth century, St. Petersburg was built expressly to advertise the triumph of enlightened absolutism at home and to display Russia's status as a major European power abroad. But for all of its neo-classical splendor, the image of imperial St. Petersburg has been consistently invoked as a critical touchstone for the expression of political discontent, social unease and spiritual anxiety. The most modern and \"intentional\" of Russian cities, Russia's northern capital has come to stand for everything that's wrong with modern life. In this seminar, we will approach St. Petersburg as a cultural text composed by an illustrious trio of Russian writers who saw the complicated history of their country through Peter's \"window to the west\" -- Nikolai Gogol, Fyodor Dostoevsky and Andrei Bely.", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(HST-AS) (HA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2895, "crseId": 370160, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "RUSSL", "catalogNbr": "2500", "titleShort": "Demons and Witches RUSSL/Film", "titleLong": "Demons and Witches in Russian Literature and Film", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 17864, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "11:40AM", "timeEnd": "12:55PM", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/06/2025", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "rvk3", "firstName": "Raissa", "middleName": "V.", "lastName": "Krivitsky"}], "pattern": "TR", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/17/2025", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/21/2025", "sessionEndDt": "05/17/2025", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "A paranoid husband believes that his wife is a witch. A man rejects vehemently the very idea of the Devil's existence, unwittingly doing so right in his face. From outright horrifying to eerily funny, always dangerous, but at times benevolent, demons, witches, and other mysterious and elusive creatures of Russian lore inhabit people's imagination and figure prominently in a number of Russian books and films. In this course, we will read and discuss fairy tales, pieces of poetry, short stories, and one of the greatest novels in Russian twentieth century literature. We will also watch several feature and animated films. \u00a0", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS) (CA-AG, LA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "All readings and class discussions are in English. To those pursuing the Russian minor,\u00a0guided reading in the original language may be added as an option.\u00a0", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2895, "crseId": 369392, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "RUSSL", "catalogNbr": "3341", "titleShort": "Short Russian Fiction 19thC", "titleLong": "Short Russian Fiction: 19th Century", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 17995, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "01:25PM", "timeEnd": "02:40PM", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/06/2025", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "np27", "firstName": "Nancy", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Pollak"}], "pattern": "MW", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/17/2025", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/21/2025", "sessionEndDt": "05/17/2025", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "The nineteenth-century Russian novel had its beginnings in a period of short fiction; it ended in another one. When Tolstoy was preparing to write Anna Karenina, he reread Pushkin's tales. Dostoevsky's characters have roots in Lermontov's fiction. The Russian novelists also wrote short works. This course focuses on the stories and tales of Pushkin, Lermontov, Gogol, Dostoevsky, Tolstoy, Chekhov, and others. It covers the nineteenth century and extends a decade or two in either direction, to the early years of modern Russian fiction in the late eighteenth century and to the final pre-revolutionary years in the early twentieth century.", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS) (CA-AG, LA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "Reading is in English translation. May be counted towards the literature requirement for the Russian Minor.", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2895, "crseId": 373573, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "RUSSL", "catalogNbr": "3389", "titleShort": "The Revolutionary as Author", "titleLong": "The Revolutionary as Author: Autobiography and Political Myth", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 9536, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "11:25AM", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/06/2025", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "sp2357", "firstName": "Sophie", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Pinkham"}], "pattern": "TR", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/17/2025", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "COML", "catalogNbr": "3389", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ENGL", "catalogNbr": "3989", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/21/2025", "sessionEndDt": "05/17/2025", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "In this course, we will read some of the most influential examples of a genre at the intersection of literature and history: the memoir of the revolutionary. Along the way, we will consider some of the fictional works (e.g. by Turgenev, Dostoevsky) that have been important in this nonfictional tradition, as well as poetry produced by the revolutionary currents we discuss. As we study the autobiographies of Russian, American, and German leftist political figures like Piotr Kropotkin, Emma Goldman, Ernst Toller, and Angela Davis, we will consider the literary methods these writers use to intertwine their own life stories with political history. How is life-writing a form of revolutionary self-fashioning? How have literary movements intersected with revolutionary writings? With special attention to the questions of gender, ethnicity, and race.", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS, HST-AS) (CA-AG, HA-AG, LA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2895, "crseId": 353928, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "RUSSL", "catalogNbr": "4492", "titleShort": "Superv Reading Russian Lit", "titleLong": "Supervised Reading in Russian Literature", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "IND", "ssrCom